Unnamed pipe or ordinary pipe

✅ fd = open(fifo, O_WRONLY);

    This line opens a named pipe (FIFO) for writing.

    fifo is a string variable that holds the path/name of the FIFO file.
    
    
    
    char *fifo = "myfifo";

That means:

    fifo is a pointer to a string

    "myfifo" is the name of the named pipe in the file system (just like a file)

    This FIFO must be created before opening using:

mkfifo myfifo

or programmatically using:

#include <sys/stat.h>
mkfifo("myfifo", 0666);





When you run this shell command, you're telling the operating system:

    📁 “Create a special file named myfifo in the current directory, which acts as a named pipe.”

🔍 In your C program:

char *fifo = "myfifo";
int fd = open(fifo, O_WRONLY);

    This tells the C program to open the file named myfifo, which you previously created using mkfifo.

    So yes — fifo must contain the exact same filename string as the name you used in the shell.
    
    
  
